75, -0.013824462890625, 0.031707763671875, 0.00949859619140625, 0.035736083984375, -0.003284454345703125, 0.00811004638671875, -0.0081329345703125, 0.03778076171875, 0.007015228271484375, -0.002017974853515625, 0.0261688232421875, -0.0300140380859375, -0.01267242431640625, 0.0037670135498046875, -0.017822265625, -0.018310546875, 0.0259246826171875, -0.0019016265869140625, 0.036346435546875, -0.02191162109375, -0.00027441978454589844, -0.004150390625, 0.00377655029296875, 0.02264404296875, -0.0159149169921875, 0.0025959014892578125, -0.0251007080078125, -0.0279998779296875, 0.04290771484375, -0.0203857421875, -0.01119232177734375, 0.0150299072265625, 0.0073699951171875, 0.0259552001953125, 0.01540374755859375, 0.047210693359375, 0.042205810546875, -0.010894775390625, -0.01068115234375, -0.00531005859375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Purpose: To develop and evaluate a locally deployed multi-agent AI system for structuring radiology reports and providing quality assurance.\n\nMaterials and Methods: In this retrospective study, 638 radiology reports from CT examinations of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis\u2014dictated by 15 board-certified radiologists in 2023 and 2024\u2014were analyzed. A multi-agent AI pipeline was developed to perform report structuring and quality assurance (QA). The system organized reports into standardized anatomical sections at the sentence level using a combination of regular expression rules and locally hosted large language models. It also identified mismatches between the Findings and Impression sections or within sections, gender-anatomy inconsistencies, and undocumented communication of critical findings. Two board-certified radiologists independently reviewed a subset of 45 reports.\n\nResults: The multi-agent system structured the Findings sections of all reports (22,270 sentences) into a predefined anatomical format while preserving the original content. The system flagged 90 reports (14.1%), most commonly for section mismatches (80 reports, 12.5%). In the radiologist evaluation, both reviewers agreed that 31 reports (69%) were correctly restructured, 2 reports (4%) were incorrectly restructured, and disagreed on 12 reports (27%). Both reviewers agreed that no clinically important information was omitted and no fabricated content was introduced. Overall QA performance was rated as \"excellent\" or \"good\" in 84% of evaluated reports, with the remainder rated as \"fair.\"\n\nConclusion: A locally deployed multi-agent AI system effectively integrated radiology report structuring and quality assurance into a unified workflow. The system demonstrated favorable performance in radiologist evaluation and may support standardization and quality assurance in radiology practice."}}